---
import { ANALYTICS_ENABLED, PRODUCT_NAME } from '../config';
import LegalPage from '../components/legal/LegalPage.astro';
import { CONFIRM, CONTACT_EMAIL, LICENSOR, LICENSOR_COUNTRY, OFFLINE_GRACE_DAYS, POLAR_PRIVACY } from '../components/legal/legal';

const toc = [
  { id: 'summary', label: 'The short version' },
  { id: 'who', label: 'Who we are' },
  { id: 'app', label: 'The app' },
  { id: 'website', label: 'This website' },
  { id: 'purchases', label: 'Purchases and licences' },
  { id: 'email', label: 'When you email us' },
  { id: 'sharing', label: 'Who else sees data' },
  { id: 'retention', label: 'How long we keep it' },
  { id: 'rights', label: 'Your rights' },
  { id: 'changes', label: 'Changes and contact' },
];
---
<LegalPage
  title={`Privacy · ${PRODUCT_NAME}`}
  heading="Privacy policy"
  description={`${PRODUCT_NAME} has no account. Here's every network call the app and this website make, and what each one sends.`}
  ogTheme="dark"
  toc={toc}
>
  <div class="legal-card" id="summary">
    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>{PRODUCT_NAME} runs on your Mac. Your repositories, code and commit history never leave it because of {PRODUCT_NAME}.</li>
      {/* Gate: no "no tracking", "no telemetry" or "only network calls" claim about the app until the network audit (AK-48) lands. */}
      <li>There's no {PRODUCT_NAME} account to create.</li>
      <li>The app makes two kinds of network call of its own: a <strong>licence check</strong> with Polar, and an <strong>update check</strong>. Both are listed below, field by field.</li>
      <li>Polar, our merchant of record, handles payment, tax and your receipt. We never see your card details.</li>
      <li>We don't sell data, and we don't use it for advertising.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2 id="who">Who we are</h2>
  <p>
    {PRODUCT_NAME} is made by {LICENSOR}, an individual developer based in {LICENSOR_COUNTRY} ("we", "us"). We are the controller for the
    personal data described here, except for payment data, which Polar controls (see <a href="#purchases">Purchases</a>).
    Contact: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="app">The app</h2>
  <h3>What stays on your Mac</h3>
  <p>
    {PRODUCT_NAME} reads and changes your repositories by running the <code>git</code> already installed on your Mac. It stores a few things locally,
    and nothing in this list is sent anywhere:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Settings and the list of recently opened repositories, in <code>~/Library/Application Support/{PRODUCT_NAME}</code>.</li>
    <li>The undo history for each repository, inside that repository's <code>.git</code> folder.</li>
    <li>Your trial start date and your licence key with its last successful check.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Network calls the app makes itself</h3>
  <div class="legal-table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Call</th><th>When</th><th>What it sends</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Licence activation and check</strong>, to Polar</td>
          <td>When you enter a licence key, then about once per session while you're online. The 14-day trial makes no call.</td>
          <td>
            Your licence key, our Polar organization ID, the activation ID for this Mac, and a device label so you can tell your Macs apart
            in the Polar customer portal. Like any internet request, it also reveals your IP address to Polar.
            It never sends your code, file names, repository names or Git identity.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Update check</strong></td>
          <td>Shortly after launch, then once a day while the app is open, and whenever you choose Check for Updates. You can turn the automatic checks off in the app menu.</td>
          <td>
            A request for the update file that matches your Mac's processor type (Apple silicon or Intel), with no cookies and no identifiers, plus the standard User-Agent header (the GitLanes version, the Electron and Chromium versions, and your macOS version). Your Mac compares the version itself, and downloads the update only if it's newer. If the update system ever needs more than that, this row changes first.
            Your IP address is visible to the server that hosts the update.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    After a successful licence check the app keeps working offline for up to {OFFLINE_GRACE_DAYS} days before it needs to check again.
  </p>

  <h3>Network calls you start</h3>
  <p>
    When you fetch, pull, push or clone, {PRODUCT_NAME} runs <code>git</code>, which connects to the remotes you configured using your own
    Git credentials. That traffic goes straight between your Mac and your Git host; it doesn't pass through us, and we can't see it.
    Links you open from the app (for example "Open on GitHub") open in your web browser.
  </p>

  <h3 id="ai">AI commit messages (off by default)</h3>
  <p>
    {PRODUCT_NAME} has no AI model or server of its own, and this feature does nothing until you turn it on and pick a provider. When you ask it
    to write a commit message, it sends the staged diff, the list of changed files and the branch name to the provider you picked. The first time
    you use a provider, nothing is sent until you have seen the exact text that will go out and agreed. Turning the feature off stops it:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Claude Code or Codex CLI:</strong> {PRODUCT_NAME} runs the tool you already have installed, signed in to your own account. The tool sends the text to its provider under that provider's terms. It never passes through us.</li>
    <li><strong>Ollama:</strong> the text goes to the Ollama server on your own Mac and stays there.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="website">This website</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No cookies.</strong> The site sets none. Your theme choice (light, dark or system) is kept in your browser's local storage and never leaves it.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Analytics:</strong> {ANALYTICS_ENABLED ? 'on' : 'off'}. If we turn analytics on, we'll use a cookieless, privacy-focused provider that
      counts page views without identifying you or following you across sites, and we'll name it here first.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Hosting:</strong> like every web server, our host receives your IP address, browser user agent and the page requested, and may
      keep them in short-lived logs for security and to keep the site running. Host: <span class="legal-placeholder">{CONFIRM}</span>.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Fonts and scripts</strong> are served from this site. We don't load third-party trackers, ad networks or social widgets.</li>
    <li><strong>Downloads</strong> are served by GitHub Releases, which sees your IP address under <a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub's privacy statement</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="purchases">Purchases and licences</h2>
  <p>
    Polar is the merchant of record for {PRODUCT_NAME}. When you buy, Polar collects your name, email address, billing country and payment
    details, works out and collects VAT or sales tax, and emails your receipt and licence key. Polar processes that data as an independent
    controller under the <a href={POLAR_PRIVACY}>Polar privacy policy</a>. Card details go to Polar's payment processor and never reach us.
  </p>
  <p>
    Polar shares with us what we need to support you: your name, email address, country, order details and your licence key with its
    activations. We use it to deliver your licence, answer support requests, handle refunds, prevent fraud and meet tax and accounting duties.
  </p>
  <p>
    We'll only email you about your purchase and licence. If we ever send product news, it will be opt-in, with an unsubscribe link in every email.
  </p>

  <h2 id="email">When you email us</h2>
  <p>
    If you write to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, we receive your email address and whatever you include. We use it only
    to reply and to fix what you reported. Please don't send repository contents or secrets unless we ask and you're comfortable doing so.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sharing">Who else sees data</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Polar</strong>: payments, tax, receipts, licence keys and the customer portal.</li>
    <li><strong>GitHub</strong>: hosts the app downloads and, once they ship, updates.</li>
    <li><strong>Our website host</strong>: serves this site. <span class="legal-placeholder">{CONFIRM}</span></li>
    <li><strong>Our email provider</strong>: stores support email. <span class="legal-placeholder">{CONFIRM}</span></li>
    <li>Authorities, only where the law requires it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Some of these providers are outside your country, including in the United States. Where the law requires it, transfers rely on the
    safeguards those providers offer, such as the EU Standard Contractual Clauses.
  </p>

  <h2 id="retention">How long we keep it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Order and licence records: as long as your licence exists, plus the period tax and accounting law requires (for example, up to 10 years in Turkey). <span class="legal-placeholder">{CONFIRM}</span></li>
    <li>Support email: up to 2 years after the conversation ends, unless it's part of an order record.</li>
    <li>Website server logs: kept by the host for a short period, typically no more than 30 days.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="rights">Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    Depending on where you live (including under the EU and UK GDPR and Turkey's KVKK), you can ask to see, correct, export or delete your personal
    data, object to or restrict how we use it, and withdraw any consent. Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> and we'll reply
    within 30 days. For payment data held by Polar, you can also contact Polar directly. You have the right to complain to your local data
    protection authority.
  </p>
  <p>
    Our legal bases are: performing our contract with you (licences, updates, support), our legitimate interests (security and fraud prevention),
    legal obligations (tax and accounting) and, for anything optional, your consent.
  </p>
  <p>{PRODUCT_NAME} isn't directed at children under 16, and we don't knowingly collect their data.</p>

  <h2 id="changes">Changes and contact</h2>
  <p>
    If we change this policy, we'll update this page. If a change affects what the app sends, we'll say so in the changelog before the app
    version that makes the change. Questions: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
  </p>
</LegalPage>
